WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:07.120
This is my first video in English, so please be&nbsp;
kind. All right, from an Italian perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:12.960
I mean, from some unknown guy who lives in Italy,&nbsp;
which is me, Colombia is quite fascinating.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:19.440
Colombia is not an easy country. We’re dealing&nbsp;
with a country that preserves one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:24.800
greatest biodiversity in the world, a stunning&nbsp;
landscape with every kind of natural habitat:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:30.560
from savannahs to rainforests, all renowned&nbsp;
for the quality of their products (coffee,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:37.440
cocoa, flowers and tropical fruits). Colombia&nbsp;
is country known for other nice things, such as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:44.480
being the birthplace of great artists and writers&nbsp;
such as Fernando Botero or Gabriel Garcia Marquez&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:49.440
(awarded the Nobel Prize for Literature&nbsp;
in 1982 in this case). This is lovely, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.760
unfortunately there is the other side of the coin,&nbsp;
that is: drug cartels, drug trafficking, violence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:01.440
and armed conflict, which are just some of the&nbsp;
key words that unfortunately show up when we talk&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:07.120
about Colombia. But let’s start with the latest&nbsp;
news: : the capture of the most wanted trafficker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.120 --> 00:01:14.080
the head of the Gulf Clan - El Cartel del Golfo&nbsp;
- Dario Antonio Usuga, better known as Otoniel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.560
now detained in a location in the northwest of&nbsp;
the country, near the border with Panama. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:24.160
capture was "the most important expedition in the&nbsp;
jungle ever seen in the military history of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:31.200
country and the hardest blow to the drug trade,&nbsp;
comparable only to the downfall of Pablo Escobar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:37.840
someone I think you know. But let's let's take&nbsp;
it step by step.. So, geographically speaking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:43.200
Colombia is one of the largest countries&nbsp;
in South America and is located in the northwestern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:48.320
part of the continent, overlooking two&nbsp;
oceans, the Pacific Ocean to the west&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:54.080
and the Atlantic Ocean to the east, thus enjoying&nbsp;
a fundamental strategic position. The territory is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:02.720
also rich in natural resources (such as gas and&nbsp;
oil), mining (nickel, emeralds and gold), energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:09.200
(coal and iron), water and of course the evergreen&nbsp;
agriculture. A country full of opportunities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.200 --> 00:02:12.640
and yet from the socio-economic point of view,&nbsp;
despite the fact that the per capita GDP is in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:18.640
the medium-high range of Latin America and&nbsp;
the economy is fairly flourishing thanks to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:23.280
exports and low foreign debt, the rates of poverty&nbsp;
and inequality in income distribution and land&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:29.200
ownership in Columbia remain very high.&nbsp;
In 2020 According to DANE, 42.5%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:35.680
of Colombians were found be in extreme poverty. If&nbsp;
we were to identify one constant that has left its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:41.120
mark on the country's troubled history almost&nbsp;
since its independence from the Spanish Empire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:46.880
in eighteen nineteen, that is undoubtedly&nbsp;
violence. Since the 19th century, the territory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:52.720
has experienced the devastating effects of no less&nbsp;
than 24 wars, including conflicts with Ecuador&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.720 --> 00:02:58.880
and Peru, as well as popular uprisings, an harsh&nbsp;
reality of violence during the 1940s and 1950s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:05.360
and an armed conflict that in the last 70 years&nbsp;
has involved various actors such as guerrillas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.360 --> 00:03:11.280
paramilitaries, drug traffickers and the&nbsp;
government itself, with the support of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:18.720
United States. in the last two centuries Colombia&nbsp;
has lived and still lives a situation of permanent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.720 --> 00:03:24.240
violence. This phenomenon, which today contributes&nbsp;
to destabilize not only the region but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:28.960
the neighboring countries, especially Venezuela&nbsp;
and Ecuador, has essentially political roots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.920
which date back to the period of the so-called&nbsp;
"violencia", which goes from 1946 to 1958,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:36.160
during which the liberal leader Jorge&nbsp;
Gaitàn was assassinated in the capital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.000
Bogotà. Gaitán is considered one of&nbsp;
the most prominent leaders of the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:45.760
century in Colombia. The founder of the socialist&nbsp;
party Unión Nacional Izquierdista Revolucionaria,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:50.800
was strongly committed to returning land to small&nbsp;
private settlers and tenants. Gaitán supported the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:55.760
rights of the peasant masses and denounced all the&nbsp;
abuses carried out by the public authorities in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.760 --> 00:04:01.440
complicity with the landowners, who in turn were&nbsp;
associated with large multinational companies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:06.320
among which we can mention the United Fruit&nbsp;
Company. On top of that, Gaitan promoted the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.320 --> 00:04:11.440
literacy of the peasants and a basic health&nbsp;
care system for all of them. A glimmer of hope&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.440 --> 00:04:17.760
destined, however, to be extinguished in 1935,&nbsp;
when the party was dissolved and its leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:23.760
decided to join the Colombian Liberal Party, while&nbsp;
maintaining a declared socialist orientation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:29.280
The more traditionalist people, however, despite&nbsp;
benefiting from the popularity of Gaitan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:34.960
did not tolerate his progressive ideas,&nbsp;
and ended up in permanent contrast. And so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:41.360
in 1948, on the eve of new elections that saw&nbsp;
him clearly in favor, Gaitan was assassinated,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:48.160
triggering a bloody popular uprising known as "El&nbsp;
Bogotazo". El Bogotazo escalated like a river in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:54.400
flood in the form of raids and mobilizations,&nbsp;
initially fomented by the Liberal Party but then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:00.000
totally out of control. Needless to say, the&nbsp;
government's response was not long in coming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:06.080
triggering a fierce clash between special police&nbsp;
forces and civilians. The outcome was more than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:11.440
200.000 thousand dead. The situation of chaos&nbsp;
that had been created saw the birth of the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:17.520
guerrilla cells: "liberales limpios", the&nbsp;
most ancient and liberal faction, opposed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.520 --> 00:05:24.320
to the "Comunes", of communist matrix. Initially,&nbsp;
both were driven by the need to defend themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.320 --> 00:05:28.880
from the wave of violence perpetrated by members&nbsp;
and sympathizers of the conservative party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.880 --> 00:05:34.800
the ones the campesinos called agents of terror&nbsp;
and repression. In the 50's it was almost taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.800 --> 00:05:41.200
for granted that the traditional parties would&nbsp;
join forces to fight a common enemy and in 1952,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.200 --> 00:05:46.160
thanks to the first military assistance treaty&nbsp;
signed between Colombia and the United States,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.160 --> 00:05:52.080
the subversive groups were hit by a powerful&nbsp;
attack from the government forces, reinforced&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:59.120
by a greater quantity of weapons and by the use of&nbsp;
new training techniques provided by the Americans.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:03.680
None of this, however, could ease the&nbsp;
internal political divisions and this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.680 --> 00:06:10.640
created the ideal situation for the military&nbsp;
coup of June 1953, the only one in the entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:17.040
republican history of Columbia. The coup was&nbsp;
organized by a general called Gustavo Pinilla,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:23.520
who proclaimed himself head of state, forcing&nbsp;
the elected president Laureano Gomez into exile.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:27.600
The first step of the new government&nbsp;
was to get to a pacification campaign,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:33.280
trying to dismantle the guerrilla cells and&nbsp;
rebuild the areas most affected by the violence.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:38.560
The guerrillas were invited to surrender and lay&nbsp;
down their weapons in exchange for a series of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:44.880
benefits , including amnesty and pardon for all&nbsp;
those political crimes committed before 1954.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:49.840
Most of the fighters accepted the conditions, with&nbsp;
the exception of the communist guerrillas who had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.840 --> 00:06:55.040
never fully trusted the peace proposals posed by&nbsp;
the governement. And with good reason I might say.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.040 --> 00:07:00.800
the following years the government was repeatedly&nbsp;
accused of not having respected the pacts, also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.800 --> 00:07:07.840
due to the murder of Guadalupe Salcedo in 1957,&nbsp;
one of the most famous guerrilla leaders, which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.840 --> 00:07:14.080
took place in circumstances never fully clarified.&nbsp;
Later, the two traditional parties, under the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:20.160
leadership of the liberal Alberto Camargo and the&nbsp;
conservative Guillermo Valencia, joined forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:26.240
to overthrow Pinilla's military dictatorship,&nbsp;
forming the system of government known as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:33.920
National Front from 1958 to 1974. The National&nbsp;
Front was set to exclude any new political force.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:39.040
It was meant to lead the the population to&nbsp;
gradually lose interest in political life and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:45.520
to abstain from voting. However, from the 1960s to&nbsp;
the present day, there were some major phenomena&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.520 --> 00:07:51.680
that took form at the same time in Colombia: the&nbsp;
emergence of guerrilla organizations, the attempts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.680 --> 00:07:57.760
to combat them both militarily and peacefully,&nbsp;
the unstoppable rise of drug trafficking, the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:04.320
appearance of paramilitarism and the systematic&nbsp;
use of kidnapping for extortion. Experts finds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:09.440
the main reason for the disastrous situation in&nbsp;
Colombia to be in the unequal distribution of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:15.200
land. The repressive actions led by the government&nbsp;
are basically a reaction to popular demands for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.200 --> 00:08:20.640
social justice. And this has seen as a consequence&nbsp;
the emergence of different armed groups:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:27.120
Among these are the FARC-EP (Revolutionary Armed&nbsp;
Forces of Colombia-People's Army) operating since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:34.380
1964 in the regions of Tolima, Cauca and Huìla&nbsp;
and to a lesser extent still existing today. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:38.640
Revolutionary Armed Forces grew in a short time&nbsp;
to the point of becoming a state within the state.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:44.800
Just to understand what this means: when in 1964&nbsp;
the government conducted the so called Operation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.800 --> 00:08:50.000
Soberania (sovereignty) to strike the guerrillas'&nbsp;
headquarters, the Revolutionary Forec’s territory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:56.960
was a region of more than 800 square kilometers&nbsp;
with the name of “Republic of Marquetalia”. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:03.200
Operation allowed the government to retake the&nbsp;
region, but failed in capturing the big fish,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:08.000
who had been informed in time of the attack.&nbsp;
The group would later reorganize itself, going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:13.520
from a mere resistance form to a revolutionary&nbsp;
armed struggle, in a hostile territory with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:19.120
absence of communication routes and adverse&nbsp;
weather conditions. Thus, the FARC succeeded&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.120 --> 00:09:25.360
in taking complete control of at least a third of&nbsp;
the country. This led the Colombian State to adopt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:31.600
a more bland approach. hey eventually signed in&nbsp;
November 2016 a peace agreement. But the oldest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.600 --> 00:09:39.120
and currently existing guerrilla fringe is instead&nbsp;
the ELN (National Liberation Army), inspired by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:45.200
Che Guevara ideals and Catholic tendencies (so&nbsp;
much so that many Spanish priests were active in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.200 --> 00:09:50.000
its ranks). Almost all the guerrilla organizations&nbsp;
still active in the country have progressively&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:53.680
abandoned their ideologies as well as the fight&nbsp;
with political aims. They're now only mainly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:59.520
involved in the production and distribution of&nbsp;
cocaine and activities such as weapon trafficking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:05.920
kidnapping and extortion. In 1980s and 1990s, the&nbsp;
scourge of paramilitarism, a true form of state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:11.680
terrorism, entered the scene. The paramilitaries&nbsp;
of the AUC (United Self-Defense Units of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.680 --> 00:10:17.360
Colombia), financed with the aim of curbing the&nbsp;
actions of the guerrillas, spread panic among the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:22.880
population. These paramilitaries were among the&nbsp;
main culprits of land grabs and brutal massacres,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:28.160
such as that of Mapiripàn, during which&nbsp;
(according to the U.S. State Department) at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.160 --> 00:10:38.160
30 civilians were brutally killed with machetes&nbsp;
and chainsaws. And who were the puppet masters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:43.920
of these executioners? It is quickly said:&nbsp;
businessmen and drug traffickers with the support&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:48.880
of the political and military authority&nbsp;
leading the country. And that’s not all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.880 --> 00:10:56.960
since the US strongly contributed to their origin&nbsp;
in the so-called Plan Lazo suggested in 1962 by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:02.960
General William Yarbrough. The Plan Lazo was aimed&nbsp;
at contrasting the guerrilla movements and crush&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.960 --> 00:11:08.800
any form of association with the Communist Party&nbsp;
in Colombia, with the help of US Mobile training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.800 --> 00:11:13.680
teams and Colombian military. The United States&nbsp;
presence is also found on other occasions: one of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.680 --> 00:11:20.720
these is the bilateral agreement of 1999, known as&nbsp;
"Plan Colombia", which provided massive economic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:26.080
and military support from the United States and a&nbsp;
strong commitment from the Colombian government to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.080 --> 00:11:31.600
allocate budget resources to military spending;&nbsp;
these conditions have allowed the creation and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:36.400
training of specialized bodies in the fight&nbsp;
against terrorism and drug trafficking. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:42.160
huge amount of money used can help us describe&nbsp;
Plan Colombia as the most important aid program in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:48.080
the entire nation's history, placing it in third&nbsp;
place among countries receiving military aid after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:53.840
Egypt and Israel. Its natural successor was Plan&nbsp;
Patriota, which consisted of a greater offensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.840 --> 00:11:59.600
against the FARC, now considered a terrorist group&nbsp;
on the same level as other paramilitaries. To all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:06.000
of this we can only add the last but not least&nbsp;
actor in the armed conflict, the state itself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:10.880
which has played a key role in the increase&nbsp;
of violence in the country. A clear example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.880 --> 00:12:16.480
is given by so-called "false positives", which&nbsp;
recalls one of the darkest recent events in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:23.440
history of the country, consisting of a series of&nbsp;
executions that took place between 2002 and 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.440 --> 00:12:28.800
under the presidency of Alvaro Velez (member of&nbsp;
the same party that is currently in government).&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:35.200
The false positives were part of a policy known as&nbsp;
"democratic security". The targets were civilians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:35.200 --> 00:12:42.800
that no one would miss, such as drug addicts,&nbsp;
mentally ill people, beggars, etc. These people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.800 --> 00:12:49.920
were registered by army officials as fighters who&nbsp;
had fallen in combat; in this way, the military&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:49.920 --> 00:12:55.680
obtained economic benefits, additional leave&nbsp;
and career promotions. The numbers point to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.240 --> 00:13:03.040
6,000 people dead. And because of the high rate&nbsp;
of impunity, in 2019 Colombia was condemned by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.040 --> 00:13:10.080
the Inter-American Court of Human Rights. However,&nbsp;
it was only in 2016 that a first effective step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.080 --> 00:13:15.600
towards internal pacification was taken, when the&nbsp;
new President of the Republic Juan Calderón and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.600 --> 00:13:21.440
the commander of the FARC Timoleòn Jimenez, signed&nbsp;
the final agreement for peace. It took the form&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.440 --> 00:13:27.440
of a long document of more than 300 pages that&nbsp;
is divided in 6 main points, including a way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.440 --> 00:13:33.200
address the problem of drugs, the demobilization&nbsp;
of the guerrillas and the much needed agrarian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:33.200 --> 00:13:38.960
reform. Reforms or not the situation in Colombia&nbsp;
unfortunately has not improved at all. Violence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.960 --> 00:13:45.120
has even worsened in many regions since the first&nbsp;
negotiations, cause the groups of paramilitaries,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.120 --> 00:13:51.600
former FARC guerrillas and drug traffickers have&nbsp;
become even stronger. In recent years the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.600 --> 00:13:55.920
has seen an increase in violent activities by&nbsp;
the many armed forces (about 30 in total) that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:55.920 --> 00:14:01.440
fight among themselves for control over the&nbsp;
territory, competing for the traffic of cocaine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.440 --> 00:14:07.920
and timber as well as illegal mining, all with&nbsp;
great repercussions on the civilians. And speaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:07.920 --> 00:14:14.080
of drugs. these substances began circulating in&nbsp;
the 70's with the first cultivations of marijuana&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:19.680
and then expanded towards cocaine and heroin along&nbsp;
with the growth of organized crime. In the 80s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.680 --> 00:14:26.240
first cartels were born, the one of Medellin, led&nbsp;
by Pablo Escobar, whose nickname was "El Patrón"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:31.600
and his rival, the Cali Cartel under the&nbsp;
leadership of the brothers Gilberto and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.600 --> 00:14:38.400
Miguel Rodriguez. After Escobar's death in 1993&nbsp;
and the arrest of the heads of the rival cartel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.400 --> 00:14:45.120
it was the Norte del Valle Cartel that established&nbsp;
supremacy over the territory. It remains however&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:45.120 --> 00:14:55.680
difficult to accurately quantify Colombian&nbsp;
cocaine production. In the early 1990s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.680 --> 00:15:00.240
Colombian laboratories were processing the&nbsp;
world's largest amount of cocaine, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.240 --> 00:15:06.960
Peru was the largest grower, producing 60% of the&nbsp;
world's product. Raw cocaine was flown from Peru&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.960 --> 00:15:12.080
to southeastern Colombia for processing and then&nbsp;
sent to the north of the country and to the west&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:16.880
coast for shipment to the United States. In the&nbsp;
latest years, Colombian coca production grew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:22.560
exponentially, with an estimated more than two&nbsp;
hundred tons produced in '99. All of this while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:29.120
Peru struggled in maintaining high numbers. In&nbsp;
other words, while Peruvian harvests declined due&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:35.520
to an efficient programme of fumigation, Colombian&nbsp;
growers increased the number of hectares dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:35.520 --> 00:15:41.600
to coca, mainly in the south-western region of&nbsp;
Putumayo. According to the CIA, in the same year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:48.560
20,000 new hectares were allocated to cultivation,&nbsp;
an increase of 20% over the previous year for a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.560 --> 00:15:50.160
total of 100,210,000 hectares. Production&nbsp;
estimates would exceed five hundred twenty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:56.000
thousand tons. These estimates were then denied&nbsp;
by the Colombian National Police, which instead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.000 --> 00:16:01.440
quantified the number of hectares cultivated with&nbsp;
coca at around one hundred thousand. To these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.440 --> 00:16:06.560
number we can add the business of opium poppy&nbsp;
cultivation. According to the U.S. anti-drug&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.560 --> 00:16:12.400
agency, criminal organizations have entered this&nbsp;
business after the dismantling of the Medellin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:12.400 --> 00:16:18.400
and Cali cartels. According to a report of the&nbsp;
same agency, but these are data to be taken with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.400 --> 00:16:25.040
caution, in those years more than 6000 hectares&nbsp;
of land were used for the production of heroin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:32.720
mainly concentrated in the Andean areas of Tolima&nbsp;
and Huila. It has been argued that about 75%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:32.720 --> 00:16:38.400
of the heroin consumed in the East Coast came&nbsp;
from Colombia, despite that on the world scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

